assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.001
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "w" stream: fputs("abc") and fflush() (the step the rules ask
           for), then write(fileno(f), "def"), then fputs("ghi"), fflush()
           and fclose() leave the file holding "abcdefghi".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/001-stream-then-fd.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.002
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "w" stream with d = dup(fileno(f)): fputs("ab"), fflush(),
           write(d, "cd"), fputs("ef"), fflush(), write(d, "gh") and fclose()
           leave the file holding "abcdefgh".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/002-dup-handle.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.003
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       With a "w" stream f and g = fdopen(dup(fileno(f)), "w"): fputs("ab", f),
           fflush(f), fputs("cd", g), fflush(g), fputs("ef", f), fflush(f),
           then fclose() of both, leave the file holding "abcdef".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/003-two-streams.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.004
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      disputed
says       On a "w" stream set _IONBF (UNRESOLVED if refused): fputc('a'),
           write(fileno(f), "b"), fputc('c'), write(fileno(f), "d") leave
           the file holding "abcd" before fclose().
note       disputed (Q-33): whether an accepted _IONBF makes the stream
           unbuffered in 2.5.1's sense is the question Q-33 asks; a FAIL
           is logged as INSPECT. The guarantee: when the application
           follows the rules, no data written is lost or duplicated, and
           all of it is written in order. The section has no change
           history; "since" is left out.
test       t/004-unbuffered.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.005
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      disputed
says       On a "w" stream set _IOLBF (UNRESOLVED if refused): fputs("a\n"),
           write(fileno(f), "b\n"), fputs("c\n"), write(fileno(f), "d\n")
           leave the file holding "a\nb\nc\nd\n" before fclose().
note       disputed (Q-33): what line buffering does is implementation-
           defined (XSH 2.5), and this case relies on it; a FAIL is
           logged as INSPECT. The guarantee: when the application
           follows the rules, no data written is lost or duplicated, and
           all of it is written in order. The section has no change
           history; "since" is left out.
test       t/005-line-buffered.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.006
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a stream opened "r+" on "0123456": fputs("XY") and fflush(), then
           write(fileno(f), "Z") (no lseek(): the stream changed the offset
           only by writing), then fclose(), leave "XYZ3456".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/006-update-stream.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.007
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On an "a" stream on a file holding "0": fputs("1"), fflush(),
           lseek(fileno(f), 0, SEEK_SET) and write(fileno(f), "2"), then
           fseek(f, 0, SEEK_SET) (the step the rules ask for after an
           explicit seek), fputs("3") and fclose() leave "0123": every
           write goes to the end.
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/007-append-stream.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.008
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       A "w" stream holding "x", flushed before fork() (the rules treat a
           fork() as a change of handle); the child writes "c" with fputs()
           and calls exit(); after waiting, the parent writes "p" with
           fputs(), fflush() and fclose(): the file holds "xcp".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/008-fork-child-writes.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.009
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       A "w" stream with "abc" pending (not flushed); fork(); the child
           calls _exit(0) at once (so it never accesses the handle); after
           waiting, the parent calls fclose(): the file holds "abc".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/009-fork-exit-not-duplicated.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.010
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       A "w" stream with "abc" pending (not flushed); fork(); the child
           calls execv() on this program (which at once calls _exit(0));
           after waiting, the parent calls fclose(): the file holds "abc".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/010-fork-exec-not-duplicated.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.011
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "r+" stream on "0123456": fflush() (the step the rules ask
           for), lseek() on its descriptor to 5 and write(fileno(f), "Q");
           then fseek(f, 1, SEEK_SET) (the step the rules ask for after an
           explicit seek), fputs("Z") and fflush(): the file holds
           "0Z234Q6". Then the descriptor moves to 6 again, and fseek(f, 2,
           SEEK_SET), the position the stream already has, still makes the
           next fputs("Y") land at 2: "0ZY34Q6".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/011-fd-then-stream-seek.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.012
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       With a "w" stream f and g = fdopen(dup(fileno(f)), "w"): fputs("ab",
           f), fputs("cd", g), fclose(f), fclose(g) leave the file holding
           "abcd" (each stream's output goes where the description's offset
           is when it is written).
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/012-close-only.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.013
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "r+" stream on "abc" read to end of file (feof() true),
           write(fileno(f), "d") with no fflush(), then fputs("e") (output
           may follow input that reached end of file) and fclose() leave
           "abcde".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/013-at-end-of-file.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.014
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "r+" stream on "0123456" (whose read-ahead may take the whole
           file): fgetc() returns '0', then fflush() (the step the rules ask
           for), then write(fileno(f), "Z") and fclose(): the file holds
           "0Z23456".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/014-reading-stream.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.015
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a stream from fdopen() of a pipe's write end: fputs("ab"),
           fflush(), write(fileno(f), "cd"), fputs("ef") and fclose(); the
           read end then yields "abcdef".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
test       t/015-pipe.c

assertion  XSH.2.5.1-stream-handles.016
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.5.1
class      required
says       On a "w" stream on f with d = dup(fileno(f)): fputs("ab"), then
           freopen("g", "w", f) (which counts as closing the stream), then
           write(d, "cd"): f holds "abcd".
note       The guarantee: when the application follows the rules, no
           data written is lost or duplicated, and all of it is written
           in order. The section has no change history; "since" is left
           out.
nomutant   freopen() has no planted faults yet; one that dropped the
           pending data would give "cd".
test       t/016-freopen-closes.c
